## Unified Firmware API Gaps / Confirmations Needed

The unified ESP32 firmware target will use the current API at
`http://192.168.1.42/trail_cam/api/...`.

Already covered by current routes:

- Onboard/local camera image upload can use:
  - `POST /api/board/media`
  - `PUT /api/board/media/{media_id}/blob`
- WiFi scan result upload can use:
  - `POST /api/observations/upload`
- Trail camera media should be uploaded directly when possible. If direct upload
  fails, the firmware should report the failure and leave the original media on
  the trail camera instead of spooling trail camera media to ESP32 SD.
- Board events can use:
  - `POST /api/board/events`

Needs server confirmation or adjustment:

- `POST /api/observations/upload` should accept BLE observations in the same
  request shape as WiFi observations, with `device_type: "BLE"` and optional BLE
  fields when available:
  - `is_randomized`
  - `manufacturer_data`
  - `adv_services`
  - `adv_service_data`
  - `tx_power`
  - `local_name`
  - `tracker_type` returned/stored after server-side classification
- BLE observation rows should store `channel`, `freq_mhz`, and `ssid` as null.
  BLE names should use `local_name`, not the WiFi `ssid` field.
- The ESP32 should send raw parsed advertisement fields only. The server should
  classify trackers server-side using the existing `air_scan` BLE classifier so
  tracker rules can change without reflashing ESP32 boards.
- BLE randomized address handling differs from WiFi locally-administered MAC
  handling. The server should trust the firmware's `is_randomized` flag or
  apply BLE logic: first address byte `& 0x40`.
- BLE manufacturer names should come from Bluetooth SIG company IDs in
  `manufacturer_data` when available, not WiFi OUI lookup.
- `POST /api/board/telemetry` should accept warning/status fields alongside
  battery and temperature readings. The firmware needs to report:
  - low SD free space warning when SD free space is below 10%
  - pending failed uploads / retry queue warning
  - low battery warning
  - scanner disabled by battery policy
  - onboard camera disabled by battery policy
  - survival mode active
- `POST /api/board/telemetry` should accept SD queue state, for example:
  - SD present/ready
  - SD total/used/free bytes
  - SD free percent
  - queued upload count
  - queued WiFi/BLE scan result count
  - queued onboard image count
  - oldest queued item age
- `POST /api/board/telemetry` should accept ESP32 internal chip temperature.
  External temperature/humidity probe support is intentionally deferred.
- The server should treat media uploads as idempotent by `sha256` plus
  `scanner_host`/`source`/`recorded_at`, so firmware can retry forever after SD
  spooling without creating duplicate media rows.

Firmware policy decisions this API must support:

- HaLow stays up and uploads immediately whenever possible.
- SD is fallback spool only:
  - save data only when immediate upload is unavailable or fails
  - delete queued files after successful upload
  - if SD pressure cleanup is needed, delete oldest WiFi/BLE scan result files
    first, then oldest onboard/local camera image files
  - do not spool trail camera media to ESP32 SD
- Scanner schedule defaults:
  - scheduled WiFi/BLE scanning disabled by default on the device
  - manual `POST /upload/observations` still runs WiFi/BLE scans on demand
  - API control available with `GET/POST /scanner/config`, `POST /scanner/enable`, and `POST /scanner/disable`
  - daytime window `07:00` through `21:00`
  - WiFi scan every 2 minutes during daytime
  - BLE scan every 2 minutes during daytime
  - WiFi scan every 15 minutes at night
  - BLE scan every 15 minutes at night
- Onboard/local camera schedule defaults:
  - enabled
  - one image every 30 minutes
  - active from `06:00` through `18:00`
- Battery policy defaults:
  - warning at or below 3.70V
  - disable WiFi/BLE scanners at or below 3.60V
  - disable scheduled onboard camera captures at or below 3.50V
  - survival mode at or below 3.40V
  - survival mode rejects trail-camera bridge requests unless an API call
    explicitly forces the request.

Board SD hardware found from prior HT-HC33 validation:

- interface: SPI SD, not SD_MMC
- MOSI: GPIO11
- CLK/SCK: GPIO15
- MISO: GPIO16
- CS: GPIO10
- validated writable mount frequency: 4 MHz

## Server-to-Board Communication Tests

The server should be able to talk to the ESP32 board over HaLow. The board does
not have a guaranteed fixed DHCP address, so server code should discover/use the
latest board registration when available. Current live validation address:

- board hostname: `trail_esp32`
- latest HaLow IP seen: `192.168.1.156`
- board HTTP base URL: `http://192.168.1.156:18080`

Minimum server-side health checks:

1. `GET http://<board-ip>:18080/status`
   - expect HTTP `200`
   - expect JSON
   - required fields:
     - `hostname`
     - `halow_connected`
     - `halow_ip`
     - `wifi_connected`
     - `battery`
     - `onboard_camera`
     - `upload`
     - `ble_stage`
   - healthy current powered-camera state should show:
     - `hostname: "trail_esp32"`
     - `halow_connected: true`
     - `ble_wake_confirmed: true` after bringup
     - `ble_stage: "wake_ok"` after bringup

2. `GET http://<board-ip>:18080/battery/status`
   - expect HTTP `200`
   - required fields:
     - `adc_mv`
     - `battery_est_v`
     - `charging_gpio15`
     - `done_gpio16`
   - warning/status aggregation should eventually include low-battery, SD, and
     upload-queue warnings in the telemetry uploaded to the server.

3. `GET http://<board-ip>:18080/onboard/status`
   - expect HTTP `200`
   - required fields:
     - `ready`
     - `enabled`
     - `interval_ms`
     - `window_start`
     - `window_end`
     - `framesize`
     - `jpeg_quality`
     - `psram_found`
     - `aec`
     - `aec_value`
   - expected default camera state:
     - `interval_ms: 1800000`
     - `window_start: "06:00"`
     - `window_end: "18:00"`
     - `framesize: "UXGA"`
     - `jpeg_quality: 8`
     - `aec: true`
     - `aec_value: 300`

4. `POST http://<board-ip>:18080/onboard/capture`
   - expect HTTP `200` with JSON metadata when capture succeeds
   - server/web GUI may pass one-shot camera tuning query args, for example:
     - `framesize=UXGA`
     - `jpeg_quality=8`
     - `aec=0`
     - `aec_value=2300`
   - one-shot settings should be restored after the capture.

5. `GET http://<board-ip>:18080/onboard/latest.jpg`
   - expect HTTP `200` and `image/jpeg` after at least one successful onboard
     capture
   - if no image exists yet, the board may return an error JSON/status.

6. `POST http://<board-ip>:18080/onboard/config`
   - server/web GUI should be able to update persistent local-camera behavior:
     - `enabled=true|false`
     - `interval_ms=1800000`
     - `start=06:00` or `start_minute=360`
     - `end=18:00` or `end_minute=1080`
     - `tz_offset_min=<minutes>`
     - `epoch=<unix_seconds>` for clock sync/testing
     - sensor fields: `framesize`, `jpeg_quality`, `brightness`, `contrast`,
       `saturation`, `sharpness`, `vflip`, `hmirror`, `awb`, `awb_gain`,
       `wb_mode`, `aec`, `aec2`, `ae_level`, `aec_value`, `agc`, `agc_gain`,
       `special_effect`

7. `POST http://<board-ip>:18080/control/bringup`
   - expect a quick accepted/queued JSON response from the board
   - server should poll `/status` until control is no longer busy
   - successful current behavior:
     - NimBLE finds target `a4:6d:d4:9e:47:32`
     - board receives `OK` notifications from `6e400004`
     - trail-camera hotspot becomes visible
     - board joins trail-camera WiFi as `192.168.8.x`
     - `/status` shows `wifi_connected: true`

8. `POST http://<board-ip>:18080/control/stream_start`
   - expect quick accepted/queued JSON response
   - server should poll `/status` and live tunnel status
   - stream success should eventually show:
     - `stream_active: true`
     - increasing `media_primary_packets` / `media_secondary_packets`
     - tunnel connected if the upstream receiver is running

9. `POST http://<board-ip>:18080/control/stream_stop`
   - expect quick accepted/queued JSON response
   - server should poll `/status` until `stream_active: false`

10. `GET http://<board-ip>:18080/camera/request?path=<camera-path>`
    or `POST http://<board-ip>:18080/camera/request`
    - use only while the trail-camera session is active
    - server can proxy camera HTTP reads/settings through the board
    - examples:
      - `/cmd/getParaSetting`
      - `/cmd/info/1`
      - `/cmd/standby/reset`

11. `GET http://<board-ip>:18080/camera/raw?path=<camera-path>`
    - use for raw camera file/media fetches when needed
    - server should prefer direct trail-camera media upload/stream when
      possible and leave failed trail-camera media on the trail camera.

12. `GET http://<board-ip>:18080/scan/wifi`
    - expect HTTP `200` only when the trail-camera WiFi session is idle
    - if camera WiFi/session is active, expect a conflict response such as
      `camera_wifi_active`
    - scheduled scanner work should pause during trail-camera bridge activity.

13. `GET http://<board-ip>:18080/upload/status`
    - expect HTTP `200`
    - required fields:
      - `attempts`
      - `successes`
      - `failures`
      - `last_status_code`
      - `last_message`
      - `queued_events`

14. `POST http://<board-ip>:18080/upload/all`
    - asks the board to upload telemetry and queued events to the server
    - current live validation succeeded against `192.168.1.42/trail_cam/api`:
      - telemetry inserted as `telemetry_id=2`
      - events inserted: `2`
      - upload queue cleared

Server-side timeout guidance:

- `/status`, `/battery/status`, `/onboard/status`, `/upload/status`: 5 seconds
- `/onboard/capture`: 20 seconds
- queued control calls: 5 seconds for the initial accepted response, then poll
  `/status`
- bringup completion polling: up to 60 seconds
- stream start polling: up to 60 seconds

## Board-to-Server API Contract

The board currently uploads to:

- host: `192.168.1.42`
- prefix: `/trail_cam`
- base API URL: `http://192.168.1.42/trail_cam/api`

Routes the board needs from the server:

1. `POST /api/board/telemetry`
   - already live-tested from the board through `/upload/all`
   - must accept:
     - `scanner_host`
     - `hostname`
     - `recorded_at` as nullable until board clock sync is finished
     - firmware identity
     - board heap/PSRAM/uptime
     - battery readings
     - ESP32 internal chip temperature
     - radio state
     - warning/status fields for SD, upload queue, and battery policy

2. `POST /api/board/events`
   - already live-tested from the board through `/upload/all`
   - must accept queued event arrays and be safe for retry

3. `POST /api/observations/upload`
   - required for scheduled WiFi and BLE scanner observations
   - must accept mixed WiFi and BLE rows
   - BLE rows should follow the `air_scan` ESP32 integration contract:
     - `device_type: "BLE"`
     - `interface: "esp32-ble"`
     - `signal_dbm`
     - `recorded_at`
     - `is_randomized`
     - `manufacturer_data`
     - `adv_services`
     - `adv_service_data`
     - `tx_power`
     - `local_name`
     - `channel: null`
     - `freq_mhz: null`
     - `ssid: null`
   - server should classify `tracker_type` from raw BLE advertisement fields.

4. `POST /api/board/media`
   - required for onboard/local camera image metadata
   - source should include `onboard`
   - server should return either a completed media record or a blob upload URL

5. `PUT /api/board/media/{media_id}/blob`
   - required for raw onboard/local camera JPEG upload
   - must be retry-safe/idempotent using `sha256` plus board/source/timestamp

6. Board clock endpoint still needed
   - firmware currently can accept `epoch=<unix_seconds>` via `/onboard/config`,
     but the board still needs a clean server-owned clock sync path
   - recommended server endpoint:
     - `GET /api/board/time`
   - recommended response:
     ```json
     {
       "ok": true,
       "epoch": 1781260800,
       "tz_offset_min": -240
     }
     ```
   - board should use this to make scheduled daylight behavior work after boot
     without manual config calls.

7. Behavior/config endpoint still needed
   - server/web GUI should eventually manage one persisted board behavior model:
     - scanner day/night schedule
     - onboard camera schedule/profile
     - battery policy thresholds
     - SD spool policy
     - upload policy
   - this can either be pushed board-side through board HTTP endpoints or exposed
     server-side as a desired-config document the board periodically pulls.
